#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n_solve

#% text
# `n_solve(eq, x)` gives numerical solutions of the equation `eq` for `x`, as a list of rule lists `[[x -> a], ...]`.

#% text
# `n_solve([eq1, eq2, ...], [x, y, ...])` solves a system of equations numerically.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# An equation is written with `==`; an expression without `==` is taken as equal to zero.

#% item
# For a polynomial in one unknown, every root is given, complex ones included, each as often as its multiplicity.

#% item
# The roots of a polynomial in one unknown are ordered by real part, then imaginary part; complex roots are `complex(re, im)` numbers. `n_solve(eq, x, reals)` keeps the real ones.

#% item
# Families of solutions of periodic equations are listed member by member (16 of them); `max_roots: inf` keeps them as families with their generated parameter, `max_roots: k` keeps the first k solutions.

#% item
# Equations that `solve` can solve exactly are solved exactly and then evaluated.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `digits: d` gives the roots as balls of `d` correct digits instead of machine reals.

#% item
# With `max_roots: k` for one equation in one unknown, when the exact solutions give fewer than k roots the others come from Newton's iteration in the complex plane from starting points around the origin, in increasing modulus.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The roots of a quadratic:

#% cell
n_solve(x^2 - 2 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.4142135623730951], [x -> 1.4142135623730951]]

#% text
# A cubic with one real root and two complex ones:

#% cell
n_solve(x^3 - x - 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -0.662358978622373 - 0.5622795120623012im], [x -> -0.662358978622373 + 0.5622795120623012im], [x -> 1.324717957244746]]

#% text
# A quintic, which has no solution by radicals:

#% cell
n_solve(x^5 - x + 1, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.1673039782614187], [x -> -0.18123244446987535 - 1.0839541013177107im], [x -> -0.18123244446987535 + 1.0839541013177107im], [x -> 0.7648844336005847 - 0.3524715460317262im], [x -> 0.7648844336005847 + 0.3524715460317262im]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# All roots of unity of order 6:

#% cell
n_solve(x^6 - 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.0], [x -> -0.5 - 0.8660254037844386im], [x -> -0.5 + 0.8660254037844386im], [x -> 0.5 - 0.8660254037844386im], [x -> 0.5 + 0.8660254037844386im], [x -> 1.0]]

#% text
# A double root is given twice:

#% cell
n_solve((x - 1)^2 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1.0], [x -> 1.0]]

#% text
# A rational equation:

#% cell
n_solve(x^2 + 1/x == 3, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.8793852415718169], [x -> 0.3472963553338607], [x -> 1.532088886237956]]

#% text
# Systems of equations:

#% cell
n_solve([x*y == 1, x + y == 3], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> 0.3819660112501051, y -> 2.618033988749895], [x -> 2.618033988749895, y -> 0.3819660112501051]]

#% cell
n_solve([x^2 + y^2 == 1, y == x], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[x -> -0.7071067811865476, y -> -0.7071067811865476], [x -> 0.7071067811865476, y -> 0.7071067811865476]]

#% text
# A transcendental equation that `solve` can invert:

#% cell
n_solve(exp(x) == 2, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 0.6931471805599453]]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# More digits:

#% cell
n_solve(x^3 - x - 1 == 0, x, digits: 20)
#% output
#= [[x -> -0.662358978622373012980454 - 0.562279512062301243899182im], [x -> -0.662358978622373012980454 + 0.562279512062301243899182im], [x -> 1.324717957244746025960909]]

#% cell
n_solve(x^2 - 2, x, digits: 50)
#% output
#= [[x -> -1.414213562373095048801688724209698078569671875376948073], [x -> 1.414213562373095048801688724209698078569671875376948073]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The real roots only, as numbers:

#% cell
n_solve(x^5 + x + 1 == 0, x) |> select(_, s => is_real(x /. s)) |> map(s => x /. s, _)
#% output
#= [-0.7548776662466927]

#% text
# The eigenvalues of a matrix as the roots of its characteristic polynomial:

#% cell
n_solve(characteristic_polynomial([[2, 1, 0], [1, 3, 1], [0, 1, 4]], x), x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1.2679491924311228], [x -> 3.0], [x -> 4.732050807568878]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same values as `n` of the exact solutions of `solve`, in another order:

#% cell
n(solve(x^3 - x - 1 == 0, x))
#% output
#= [[x -> 1.324717957244746], [x -> -0.662358978622373 - 0.5622795120623012im], [x -> -0.662358978622373 + 0.5622795120623012im]]

#% text
# For an equation that mixes the unknown inside and outside a function, `find_root` searches from a starting point:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.7390851332151607]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Equations such as cos(x) = x are not solved:

#% cell
n_solve(cos(x) == x, x)
#% output
#! failure(unsolved, solve, "the unknown occurs both inside and outside a function")

#% text
# Only the principal solution of a periodic equation is given:

#% cell
n_solve(sin(x) == 1/2, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 0.5235987755982988], [x -> 2.6179938779914944], [x -> -3.665191429188092], [x -> 6.806784082777885], [x -> -5.759586531581287], [x -> 8.901179185171081], [x -> -9.948376736367678], [x -> 13.08996938995747], [x -> -12.042771838760874], [x -> 15.184364492350667], [x -> -16.231562043547264], [x -> 19.373154697137057], [x -> -18.32595714594046], [x -> 21.467549799530254], [x -> -22.51474735072685], [x -> 25.656340004316643]]

#% text
# Widely separated roots lose accuracy: the roots of x^2 - 10^10 x + 1 are 10^10 and, since their product is 1, 10^-10, but the small one comes out wrong, even with more digits.

#% cell
n_solve(x^2 - 1e10*x + 1 == 0, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1.862645149230957e-9], [x -> 10000000000.0]]

#% cell
n_solve(x^2 - 1e10*x + 1 == 0, x, digits: 20)
#% output
#= [[x -> 1.862645149230957e-9], [x -> 10000000000.0]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `find_root`, `roots`, `root`, `n`
